    INSERT INTO mcqs (topic_id, question, options, correct_index, explanation) VALUES
    (v_topic_id, 'A 82-year-old male named James presents for a routine health maintenance visit. Blood pressure is 165/70 mmHg on three separate measurements. The pulse pressure is calculated at 95 mmHg. What is the primary pathophysiological mechanism underlying isolated systolic hypertension (ISH) in older adults?', '["Primary aldosteronism causing sodium and water retention with proportional elevation of both systolic and diastolic pressures", "Increased cardiac output from hyperthyroidism-induced tachycardia and elevated stroke volume", "Excessive renin secretion by the juxtaglomerular apparatus leading to angiotensin II-mediated vasoconstriction of the peripheral resistance arterioles", "Bilateral renal artery stenosis causing renovascular hypertension with equal elevation of systolic and diastolic blood pressures", "Progressive loss of elastin fibers and increased collagen deposition in the aortic wall and large conduit arteries, leading to arterial stiffness, reduced Windkessel function, and augmented systolic pressure with preserved or decreased diastolic pressure"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Isolated systolic hypertension (ISH)** is the most common form of hypertension in adults over 60. The primary pathophysiology is **age-related arterial stiffening** due to: (1) **Loss of elastin fibers** and replacement by **collagen** in the aortic media, (2) **Calcification of the arterial wall** (arteriosclerosis), and (3) **Endothelial dysfunction** with reduced nitric oxide bioavailability. The aorta normally acts as a Windkessel (elastic reservoir), expanding during systole and recoiling during diastole to maintain continuous flow. With stiffening, the aorta loses this buffering capacity, resulting in **elevated systolic pressure** (augmented forward wave) and **decreased or unchanged diastolic pressure** (loss of elastic recoil), thereby **widening the pulse pressure**. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 37; ACC/AHA 2017 Hypertension Guidelines; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
