    (v_topic_id, 'A 76-year-old female named Florence presents for a routine health maintenance visit. Blood pressure is 178/72 mmHg on three separate measurements. The pulse pressure is calculated at 106 mmHg. What is the primary pathophysiological mechanism underlying isolated systolic hypertension (ISH) in older adults?', '["Progressive loss of elastin fibers and increased collagen deposition in the aortic wall and large conduit arteries, leading to arterial stiffness, reduced Windkessel function, and augmented systolic pressure with preserved or decreased diastolic pressure", "Increased cardiac output from hyperthyroidism-induced tachycardia and elevated stroke volume", "Bilateral renal artery stenosis causing renovascular hypertension with equal elevation of systolic and diastolic blood pressures", "Excessive renin secretion by the juxtaglomerular apparatus leading to angiotensin II-mediated vasoconstriction of the peripheral resistance arterioles", "Primary aldosteronism causing sodium and water retention with proportional elevation of both systolic and diastolic pressures"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Isolated systolic hypertension (ISH)** is the most common form of hypertension in adults over 60. The primary pathophysiology is **age-related arterial stiffening** due to: (1) **Loss of elastin fibers** and replacement by **collagen** in the aortic media, (2) **Calcification of the arterial wall** (arteriosclerosis), and (3) **Endothelial dysfunction** with reduced nitric oxide bioavailability. The aorta normally acts as a Windkessel (elastic reservoir), expanding during systole and recoiling during diastole to maintain continuous flow. With stiffening, the aorta loses this buffering capacity, resulting in **elevated systolic pressure** (augmented forward wave) and **decreased or unchanged diastolic pressure** (loss of elastic recoil), thereby **widening the pulse pressure**. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 37; ACC/AHA 2017 Hypertension Guidelines; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-year-old female named Shirley has blood pressure readings of 158/65 mmHg confirmed on three clinic visits. According to the 2017 ACC/AHA Hypertension Guidelines, which of the following correctly classifies this patient''s blood pressure and defines isolated systolic hypertension?', '["Normal blood pressure; no treatment is indicated because diastolic pressure is within normal limits", "Stage 2 hypertension (SBP >= 140 mmHg); isolated systolic hypertension is defined as SBP >= 130 mmHg with DBP < 80 mmHg, and it is the predominant pattern of hypertension in adults over 60 years of age", "Prehypertension with no cardiovascular risk; observation alone for 5 years before initiating any therapy", "White coat hypertension; the elevated systolic reading is attributable to the clinic environment and the diastolic value confirms this", "Hypertensive urgency requiring immediate hospitalization and intravenous nitroprusside infusion"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Per the **2017 ACC/AHA Hypertension Guidelines**: Normal BP is < 120/80, Elevated BP is 120-129/<80, Stage 1 HTN is 130-139 or 80-89, and **Stage 2 HTN is >= 140 or >= 90**. **Isolated systolic hypertension (ISH)** is specifically defined as **SBP >= 130 mmHg with DBP < 80 mmHg**. ISH is the **predominant hypertension pattern in adults over age 60**, affecting over 65% of hypertensive older adults. The widened pulse pressure (SBP - DBP) reflects arterial stiffness. ISH confers significant cardiovascular risk, including stroke, myocardial infarction, heart failure, and chronic kidney disease, and **must be treated**. (Ref: ACC/AHA 2017 HTN Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 37; Master the Boards Step 2 CK)'),
