    (v_topic_id, 'A 88-year-old female named Shirley with isolated systolic hypertension has a blood pressure of 175/74 mmHg, yielding a pulse pressure of 101 mmHg. Which of the following statements regarding pulse pressure in older adults is most accurate?', '["A pulse pressure above 40 mmHg is protective against coronary artery disease because it ensures adequate coronary perfusion during diastole", "An elevated pulse pressure (> 60 mmHg) is an independent predictor of cardiovascular events including stroke, myocardial infarction, and heart failure, and reflects the degree of large-artery (aortic) stiffness", "Elevated pulse pressure is exclusively caused by aortic valve regurgitation and has no association with age-related vascular changes", "Pulse pressure is clinically irrelevant and has no independent prognostic value beyond systolic and diastolic pressures individually", "Lowering pulse pressure below 20 mmHg is the primary therapeutic goal in geriatric hypertension management"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Pulse pressure (PP = SBP - DBP)** is an important independent cardiovascular risk marker in older adults. A normal resting PP is approximately 40 mmHg. In ISH, the PP is characteristically **widened (often > 60-80 mmHg)** due to arterial stiffening. Elevated PP independently predicts: (1) **Stroke**, (2) **Myocardial infarction**, (3) **Heart failure**, (4) **All-cause mortality**, and (5) **Aortic root dilation**. The Framingham Heart Study demonstrated that in adults over 60, **pulse pressure was a stronger predictor of coronary heart disease than either systolic or diastolic pressure alone**. (Ref: Framingham Heart Study; Hazzard''s Ch. 37; ACC/AHA 2017 Guidelines)'),
