    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-year-old female named Alice with isolated systolic hypertension has a blood pressure of 178/72 mmHg, yielding a pulse pressure of 106 mmHg. Which of the following statements regarding pulse pressure in older adults is most accurate?', '["A pulse pressure above 40 mmHg is protective against coronary artery disease because it ensures adequate coronary perfusion during diastole", "Lowering pulse pressure below 20 mmHg is the primary therapeutic goal in geriatric hypertension management", "Pulse pressure is clinically irrelevant and has no independent prognostic value beyond systolic and diastolic pressures individually", "Elevated pulse pressure is exclusively caused by aortic valve regurgitation and has no association with age-related vascular changes", "An elevated pulse pressure (> 60 mmHg) is an independent predictor of cardiovascular events including stroke, myocardial infarction, and heart failure, and reflects the degree of large-artery (aortic) stiffness"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Pulse pressure (PP = SBP - DBP)** is an important independent cardiovascular risk marker in older adults. A normal resting PP is approximately 40 mmHg. In ISH, the PP is characteristically **widened (often > 60-80 mmHg)** due to arterial stiffening. Elevated PP independently predicts: (1) **Stroke**, (2) **Myocardial infarction**, (3) **Heart failure**, (4) **All-cause mortality**, and (5) **Aortic root dilation**. The Framingham Heart Study demonstrated that in adults over 60, **pulse pressure was a stronger predictor of coronary heart disease than either systolic or diastolic pressure alone**. (Ref: Framingham Heart Study; Hazzard''s Ch. 37; ACC/AHA 2017 Guidelines)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 78-year-old female named Helen is diagnosed with isolated systolic hypertension (BP 186/72 mmHg). She has no diabetes, chronic kidney disease, or heart failure. After counseling on lifestyle modifications (DASH diet, sodium restriction, exercise), which of the following pharmacological agents is the most appropriate first-line therapy?', '["An alpha-1 adrenergic blocker (e.g., doxazosin) as first-line because it selectively reduces systolic pressure without affecting diastolic pressure", "A centrally acting alpha-2 agonist (e.g., clonidine) as the preferred agent due to its favorable side-effect profile in the elderly", "A direct renin inhibitor (e.g., aliskiren) combined with an ACE inhibitor for synergistic blockade of the RAAS system", "A short-acting beta-blocker (e.g., propranolol) as first-line monotherapy because it directly reduces arterial stiffness", "A thiazide or thiazide-like diuretic (e.g., chlorthalidone) or a long-acting dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker (e.g., nifedipine extended-release); both have the strongest evidence for reducing cardiovascular events in older adults with ISH"]'::jsonb, 4, 'For **isolated systolic hypertension in older adults without compelling indications** (diabetes, CKD, heart failure), the strongest evidence-based first-line agents are: (1) **Thiazide or thiazide-like diuretics** (chlorthalidone preferred over HCTZ due to longer duration and superior outcome data from ALLHAT and SHEP trials), and (2) **Long-acting dihydropyridine CCBs** (amlodipine, supported by the Syst-Eur trial). ACE inhibitors and ARBs are also first-line options, especially with compelling indications. **Beta-blockers are NOT recommended as first-line** for uncomplicated ISH in older adults (inferior stroke prevention). Alpha-blockers are associated with orthostatic hypotension and heart failure risk (ALLHAT doxazosin arm). (Ref: ACC/AHA 2017; SHEP Trial; Syst-Eur Trial; ALLHAT; Hazzard''s Ch. 37)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 90-year-old female named Dorothy is diagnosed with isolated systolic hypertension (BP 152/72 mmHg). She has no diabetes, chronic kidney disease, or heart failure. After counseling on lifestyle modifications (DASH diet, sodium restriction, exercise), which of the following pharmacological agents is the most appropriate first-line therapy?', '["A short-acting beta-blocker (e.g., propranolol) as first-line monotherapy because it directly reduces arterial stiffness", "An alpha-1 adrenergic blocker (e.g., terazosin) as first-line because it selectively reduces systolic pressure without affecting diastolic pressure", "A direct renin inhibitor (e.g., aliskiren) combined with an ACE inhibitor for synergistic blockade of the RAAS system", "A thiazide or thiazide-like diuretic (e.g., chlorthalidone) or a long-acting dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker (e.g., amlodipine); both have the strongest evidence for reducing cardiovascular events in older adults with ISH", "A centrally acting alpha-2 agonist (e.g., clonidine) as the preferred agent due to its favorable side-effect profile in the elderly"]'::jsonb, 3, 'For **isolated systolic hypertension in older adults without compelling indications** (diabetes, CKD, heart failure), the strongest evidence-based first-line agents are: (1) **Thiazide or thiazide-like diuretics** (chlorthalidone preferred over HCTZ due to longer duration and superior outcome data from ALLHAT and SHEP trials), and (2) **Long-acting dihydropyridine CCBs** (amlodipine, supported by the Syst-Eur trial). ACE inhibitors and ARBs are also first-line options, especially with compelling indications. **Beta-blockers are NOT recommended as first-line** for uncomplicated ISH in older adults (inferior stroke prevention). Alpha-blockers are associated with orthostatic hypotension and heart failure risk (ALLHAT doxazosin arm). (Ref: ACC/AHA 2017; SHEP Trial; Syst-Eur Trial; ALLHAT; Hazzard''s Ch. 37)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 94-year-old male named Henry is diagnosed with isolated systolic hypertension (BP 172/76 mmHg). He has no diabetes, chronic kidney disease, or heart failure. After counseling on lifestyle modifications (DASH diet, sodium restriction, exercise), which of the following pharmacological agents is the most appropriate first-line therapy?', '["An alpha-1 adrenergic blocker (e.g., terazosin) as first-line because it selectively reduces systolic pressure without affecting diastolic pressure", "A direct renin inhibitor (e.g., aliskiren) combined with an ACE inhibitor for synergistic blockade of the RAAS system", "A centrally acting alpha-2 agonist (e.g., clonidine) as the preferred agent due to its favorable side-effect profile in the elderly", "A thiazide or thiazide-like diuretic (e.g., hydrochlorothiazide) or a long-acting dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker (e.g., nifedipine extended-release); both have the strongest evidence for reducing cardiovascular events in older adults with ISH", "A short-acting beta-blocker (e.g., propranolol) as first-line monotherapy because it directly reduces arterial stiffness"]'::jsonb, 3, 'For **isolated systolic hypertension in older adults without compelling indications** (diabetes, CKD, heart failure), the strongest evidence-based first-line agents are: (1) **Thiazide or thiazide-like diuretics** (chlorthalidone preferred over HCTZ due to longer duration and superior outcome data from ALLHAT and SHEP trials), and (2) **Long-acting dihydropyridine CCBs** (amlodipine, supported by the Syst-Eur trial). ACE inhibitors and ARBs are also first-line options, especially with compelling indications. **Beta-blockers are NOT recommended as first-line** for uncomplicated ISH in older adults (inferior stroke prevention). Alpha-blockers are associated with orthostatic hypotension and heart failure risk (ALLHAT doxazosin arm). (Ref: ACC/AHA 2017; SHEP Trial; Syst-Eur Trial; ALLHAT; Hazzard''s Ch. 37)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 78-year-old male named Charles is diagnosed with isolated systolic hypertension (BP 158/70 mmHg). He has no diabetes, chronic kidney disease, or heart failure. After counseling on lifestyle modifications (DASH diet, sodium restriction, exercise), which of the following pharmacological agents is the most appropriate first-line therapy?', '["A direct renin inhibitor (e.g., aliskiren) combined with an ACE inhibitor for synergistic blockade of the RAAS system", "An alpha-1 adrenergic blocker (e.g., prazosin) as first-line because it selectively reduces systolic pressure without affecting diastolic pressure", "A short-acting beta-blocker (e.g., propranolol) as first-line monotherapy because it directly reduces arterial stiffness", "A centrally acting alpha-2 agonist (e.g., clonidine) as the preferred agent due to its favorable side-effect profile in the elderly", "A thiazide or thiazide-like diuretic (e.g., chlorthalidone) or a long-acting dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker (e.g., nifedipine extended-release); both have the strongest evidence for reducing cardiovascular events in older adults with ISH"]'::jsonb, 4, 'For **isolated systolic hypertension in older adults without compelling indications** (diabetes, CKD, heart failure), the strongest evidence-based first-line agents are: (1) **Thiazide or thiazide-like diuretics** (chlorthalidone preferred over HCTZ due to longer duration and superior outcome data from ALLHAT and SHEP trials), and (2) **Long-acting dihydropyridine CCBs** (amlodipine, supported by the Syst-Eur trial). ACE inhibitors and ARBs are also first-line options, especially with compelling indications. **Beta-blockers are NOT recommended as first-line** for uncomplicated ISH in older adults (inferior stroke prevention). Alpha-blockers are associated with orthostatic hypotension and heart failure risk (ALLHAT doxazosin arm). (Ref: ACC/AHA 2017; SHEP Trial; Syst-Eur Trial; ALLHAT; Hazzard''s Ch. 37)'),
