    (v_topic_id, 'A 86-year-old male named George is diagnosed with isolated systolic hypertension (BP 178/76 mmHg). He has no diabetes, chronic kidney disease, or heart failure. After counseling on lifestyle modifications (DASH diet, sodium restriction, exercise), which of the following pharmacological agents is the most appropriate first-line therapy?', '["A centrally acting alpha-2 agonist (e.g., clonidine) as the preferred agent due to its favorable side-effect profile in the elderly", "A thiazide or thiazide-like diuretic (e.g., indapamide) or a long-acting dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker (e.g., amlodipine); both have the strongest evidence for reducing cardiovascular events in older adults with ISH", "A short-acting beta-blocker (e.g., propranolol) as first-line monotherapy because it directly reduces arterial stiffness", "A direct renin inhibitor (e.g., aliskiren) combined with an ACE inhibitor for synergistic blockade of the RAAS system", "An alpha-1 adrenergic blocker (e.g., doxazosin) as first-line because it selectively reduces systolic pressure without affecting diastolic pressure"]'::jsonb, 1, 'For **isolated systolic hypertension in older adults without compelling indications** (diabetes, CKD, heart failure), the strongest evidence-based first-line agents are: (1) **Thiazide or thiazide-like diuretics** (chlorthalidone preferred over HCTZ due to longer duration and superior outcome data from ALLHAT and SHEP trials), and (2) **Long-acting dihydropyridine CCBs** (amlodipine, supported by the Syst-Eur trial). ACE inhibitors and ARBs are also first-line options, especially with compelling indications. **Beta-blockers are NOT recommended as first-line** for uncomplicated ISH in older adults (inferior stroke prevention). Alpha-blockers are associated with orthostatic hypotension and heart failure risk (ALLHAT doxazosin arm). (Ref: ACC/AHA 2017; SHEP Trial; Syst-Eur Trial; ALLHAT; Hazzard''s Ch. 37)'),
