    (v_topic_id, 'A 72-year-old female named Florence is diagnosed with isolated systolic hypertension (BP 175/62 mmHg). She has no diabetes, chronic kidney disease, or heart failure. After counseling on lifestyle modifications (DASH diet, sodium restriction, exercise), which of the following pharmacological agents is the most appropriate first-line therapy?', '["A direct renin inhibitor (e.g., aliskiren) combined with an ACE inhibitor for synergistic blockade of the RAAS system", "A centrally acting alpha-2 agonist (e.g., clonidine) as the preferred agent due to its favorable side-effect profile in the elderly", "A thiazide or thiazide-like diuretic (e.g., hydrochlorothiazide) or a long-acting dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker (e.g., amlodipine); both have the strongest evidence for reducing cardiovascular events in older adults with ISH", "An alpha-1 adrenergic blocker (e.g., doxazosin) as first-line because it selectively reduces systolic pressure without affecting diastolic pressure", "A short-acting beta-blocker (e.g., propranolol) as first-line monotherapy because it directly reduces arterial stiffness"]'::jsonb, 2, 'For **isolated systolic hypertension in older adults without compelling indications** (diabetes, CKD, heart failure), the strongest evidence-based first-line agents are: (1) **Thiazide or thiazide-like diuretics** (chlorthalidone preferred over HCTZ due to longer duration and superior outcome data from ALLHAT and SHEP trials), and (2) **Long-acting dihydropyridine CCBs** (amlodipine, supported by the Syst-Eur trial). ACE inhibitors and ARBs are also first-line options, especially with compelling indications. **Beta-blockers are NOT recommended as first-line** for uncomplicated ISH in older adults (inferior stroke prevention). Alpha-blockers are associated with orthostatic hypotension and heart failure risk (ALLHAT doxazosin arm). (Ref: ACC/AHA 2017; SHEP Trial; Syst-Eur Trial; ALLHAT; Hazzard''s Ch. 37)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 70-year-old male named Frank with isolated systolic hypertension and no history of diabetes or stroke is being managed with hydrochlorothiazide. His current BP is 148/78 mmHg. Regarding blood pressure targets in older adults, which of the following statements best reflects current evidence-based recommendations?', '["Blood pressure should never be treated in adults over 80 years of age because the risks of pharmacotherapy always outweigh the benefits", "The SPRINT trial demonstrated that intensive BP control (target SBP < 120 mmHg) in adults >= 75 years significantly reduced major cardiovascular events and all-cause mortality compared to standard control (target SBP < 140 mmHg), but with increased rates of hypotension, syncope, acute kidney injury, and electrolyte abnormalities", "All older adults should have a BP target of < 160/90 mmHg, as lowering below this threshold provides no additional benefit", "The only proven benefit of treating ISH in older adults is stroke prevention; there is no mortality benefit regardless of target", "The SPRINT trial excluded all patients over age 75, so there is no evidence to guide intensive BP targets in the very elderly"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The landmark **SPRINT trial (2015)** randomized 9,361 adults (including a prespecified subgroup of 2,636 adults aged >= 75 years, the **SPRINT-SENIOR** cohort) to intensive (SBP < 120 mmHg) vs. standard (SBP < 140 mmHg) blood pressure control. In the >= 75 subgroup, intensive treatment resulted in: (1) **34% reduction in major cardiovascular events** (MI, ACS, stroke, heart failure, CV death), and (2) **33% reduction in all-cause mortality**. However, intensive treatment increased the rates of **hypotension, syncope, acute kidney injury, and electrolyte abnormalities**. These findings support individualized, cautious BP lowering in fit older adults, while balancing the risks of overtreatment in frail patients. (Ref: SPRINT Trial, NEJM 2015; SPRINT-SENIOR, JAMA 2016; ACC/AHA 2017 Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 37)'),
