    (v_topic_id, 'A 76-year-old female named Irene with isolated systolic hypertension and no history of diabetes or stroke is being managed with indapamide. Her current BP is 148/68 mmHg. Regarding blood pressure targets in older adults, which of the following statements best reflects current evidence-based recommendations?', '["The SPRINT trial demonstrated that intensive BP control (target SBP < 120 mmHg) in adults >= 75 years significantly reduced major cardiovascular events and all-cause mortality compared to standard control (target SBP < 140 mmHg), but with increased rates of hypotension, syncope, acute kidney injury, and electrolyte abnormalities", "The SPRINT trial excluded all patients over age 75, so there is no evidence to guide intensive BP targets in the very elderly", "Blood pressure should never be treated in adults over 80 years of age because the risks of pharmacotherapy always outweigh the benefits", "The only proven benefit of treating ISH in older adults is stroke prevention; there is no mortality benefit regardless of target", "All older adults should have a BP target of < 160/90 mmHg, as lowering below this threshold provides no additional benefit"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The landmark **SPRINT trial (2015)** randomized 9,361 adults (including a prespecified subgroup of 2,636 adults aged >= 75 years, the **SPRINT-SENIOR** cohort) to intensive (SBP < 120 mmHg) vs. standard (SBP < 140 mmHg) blood pressure control. In the >= 75 subgroup, intensive treatment resulted in: (1) **34% reduction in major cardiovascular events** (MI, ACS, stroke, heart failure, CV death), and (2) **33% reduction in all-cause mortality**. However, intensive treatment increased the rates of **hypotension, syncope, acute kidney injury, and electrolyte abnormalities**. These findings support individualized, cautious BP lowering in fit older adults, while balancing the risks of overtreatment in frail patients. (Ref: SPRINT Trial, NEJM 2015; SPRINT-SENIOR, JAMA 2016; ACC/AHA 2017 Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 37)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 82-year-old male named Albert with isolated systolic hypertension and stable coronary artery disease is on three antihypertensive medications. His current BP is 108/52 mmHg. He reports new-onset dizziness, fatigue, and near-syncopal episodes. Which of the following best describes the risk of excessively lowering diastolic blood pressure in older adults with coronary artery disease?', '["The J-curve phenomenon only applies to patients under 50 years of age and is irrelevant in geriatric populations", "The J-curve phenomenon: when diastolic BP is lowered below approximately 60-65 mmHg, coronary perfusion pressure decreases (because the coronary arteries fill primarily during diastole), increasing the risk of myocardial ischemia, infarction, and mortality", "Low diastolic pressure is always beneficial because it reduces left ventricular afterload and myocardial oxygen demand", "Diastolic BP should be lowered to < 40 mmHg in all elderly patients to maximize tissue oxygen extraction", "Diastolic pressure has no effect on coronary perfusion because the coronary arteries fill exclusively during systole"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The **J-curve phenomenon** is a critical concept in geriatric cardiology. Coronary arteries are perfused predominantly during **diastole** (when the aortic valve is closed and the myocardium relaxes). In older adults with ISH and widened pulse pressure, aggressive antihypertensive therapy can lower the **diastolic BP excessively** (< 60-65 mmHg), reducing the coronary perfusion pressure gradient and paradoxically **increasing the risk of myocardial ischemia and infarction**. This is especially dangerous in patients with existing coronary artery disease or left ventricular hypertrophy. Clinical wisdom: **treat the systolic, but watch the diastolic**. Most guidelines recommend avoiding DBP < 60 mmHg in patients with CAD. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 37; ACC/AHA 2017; Messerli FH et al., JACC 2006; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 84-year-old male named Robert with isolated systolic hypertension and stable coronary artery disease is on three antihypertensive medications. His current BP is 108/52 mmHg. He reports new-onset dizziness, fatigue, and near-syncopal episodes. Which of the following best describes the risk of excessively lowering diastolic blood pressure in older adults with coronary artery disease?', '["The J-curve phenomenon only applies to patients under 50 years of age and is irrelevant in geriatric populations", "Low diastolic pressure is always beneficial because it reduces left ventricular afterload and myocardial oxygen demand", "The J-curve phenomenon: when diastolic BP is lowered below approximately 60-65 mmHg, coronary perfusion pressure decreases (because the coronary arteries fill primarily during diastole), increasing the risk of myocardial ischemia, infarction, and mortality", "Diastolic BP should be lowered to < 40 mmHg in all elderly patients to maximize tissue oxygen extraction", "Diastolic pressure has no effect on coronary perfusion because the coronary arteries fill exclusively during systole"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The **J-curve phenomenon** is a critical concept in geriatric cardiology. Coronary arteries are perfused predominantly during **diastole** (when the aortic valve is closed and the myocardium relaxes). In older adults with ISH and widened pulse pressure, aggressive antihypertensive therapy can lower the **diastolic BP excessively** (< 60-65 mmHg), reducing the coronary perfusion pressure gradient and paradoxically **increasing the risk of myocardial ischemia and infarction**. This is especially dangerous in patients with existing coronary artery disease or left ventricular hypertrophy. Clinical wisdom: **treat the systolic, but watch the diastolic**. Most guidelines recommend avoiding DBP < 60 mmHg in patients with CAD. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 37; ACC/AHA 2017; Messerli FH et al., JACC 2006; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
