    (v_topic_id, 'A 90-year-old male named Ralph has consistently elevated clinic blood pressure readings (175/74 mmHg), but he has no signs of target organ damage despite 10 years of apparent hypertension. On physical examination, the radial artery remains palpable (though pulseless) after the blood pressure cuff is inflated above the systolic reading. This finding is most consistent with which of the following conditions?', '["Pseudohypertension due to severe arterial calcification (Monckeberg medial calcific sclerosis); the calcified artery cannot be fully compressed by the cuff, leading to falsely elevated sphygmomanometric readings; the finding described is a positive Osler maneuver", "Secondary hypertension due to pheochromocytoma; the palpable artery reflects chronically elevated catecholamine levels", "White coat hypertension confirmed by the normal exam; no further evaluation is needed", "Essential hypertension with early end-organ damage masked by compensatory cardiac remodeling", "True resistant hypertension requiring the addition of a fourth antihypertensive agent and referral for renal denervation"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Pseudohypertension** is a condition in which blood pressure measured by standard sphygmomanometry is **falsely elevated** due to **heavily calcified, non-compressible arteries** (Monckeberg medial calcific sclerosis). The stiffened arterial wall requires higher cuff pressures to compress, leading to spuriously high readings. The **Osler maneuver** is a bedside test: inflate the BP cuff above the systolic reading — if the **radial or brachial artery remains palpable** (hardened, cord-like, but pulseless) after cuff occlusion, the test is **positive**, suggesting pseudohypertension. True diagnosis is confirmed by **intra-arterial (direct) blood pressure measurement**, which will show normal pressures. Treating pseudohypertension aggressively causes **dangerous iatrogenic hypotension**. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 37; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Master the Boards Step 2 CK)'),
