    (v_topic_id, 'A 92-year-old male named Paul has consistently elevated clinic blood pressure readings (162/65 mmHg), but he has no signs of target organ damage despite 3 years of apparent hypertension. On physical examination, the radial artery remains palpable (though pulseless) after the blood pressure cuff is inflated above the systolic reading. This finding is most consistent with which of the following conditions?', '["White coat hypertension confirmed by the normal exam; no further evaluation is needed", "Essential hypertension with early end-organ damage masked by compensatory cardiac remodeling", "Secondary hypertension due to pheochromocytoma; the palpable artery reflects chronically elevated catecholamine levels", "True resistant hypertension requiring the addition of a fourth antihypertensive agent and referral for renal denervation", "Pseudohypertension due to severe arterial calcification (Monckeberg medial calcific sclerosis); the calcified artery cannot be fully compressed by the cuff, leading to falsely elevated sphygmomanometric readings; the finding described is a positive Osler maneuver"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Pseudohypertension** is a condition in which blood pressure measured by standard sphygmomanometry is **falsely elevated** due to **heavily calcified, non-compressible arteries** (Monckeberg medial calcific sclerosis). The stiffened arterial wall requires higher cuff pressures to compress, leading to spuriously high readings. The **Osler maneuver** is a bedside test: inflate the BP cuff above the systolic reading — if the **radial or brachial artery remains palpable** (hardened, cord-like, but pulseless) after cuff occlusion, the test is **positive**, suggesting pseudohypertension. True diagnosis is confirmed by **intra-arterial (direct) blood pressure measurement**, which will show normal pressures. Treating pseudohypertension aggressively causes **dangerous iatrogenic hypotension**. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 37; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Master the Boards Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 86-year-old female named Louise has office blood pressure readings consistently above 150/80 mmHg across 4 clinic visits. However, her home blood pressure log shows readings consistently below 125/75 mmHg. She has no target organ damage. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step to distinguish white coat hypertension from sustained (masked) hypertension?', '["Refer for urgent cardiac catheterization to evaluate for coronary artery disease as the cause of the elevated readings", "Order 24-hour ambulatory blood pressure monitoring (ABPM); ABPM is the gold standard for diagnosing white coat hypertension, confirming true mean BP, and identifying nocturnal dipping or non-dipping patterns", "Immediately start indapamide based on the elevated clinic readings alone, as office BP is always more accurate than home measurements", "Dismiss the elevated readings entirely and discontinue monitoring because the patient reports no symptoms", "Prescribe an anxiolytic (benzodiazepine) before each clinic visit to eliminate the white coat effect and obtain a true reading"]'::jsonb, 1, '**White coat hypertension (WCH)** occurs in 15-30% of older adults and is defined as elevated BP in the clinical setting (>= 130/80 mmHg) with normal out-of-office BP (< 130/80 by ABPM or < 135/85 by home BP). **24-hour ambulatory blood pressure monitoring (ABPM)** is the **gold standard** for: (1) Diagnosing white coat hypertension (elevated office, normal ambulatory), (2) Detecting **masked hypertension** (normal office, elevated ambulatory — which carries high cardiovascular risk), (3) Evaluating **nocturnal dipping** (normal >= 10% SBP drop at night; non-dipping is an independent CV risk factor). WCH carries lower cardiovascular risk than sustained hypertension, but still requires periodic monitoring. (Ref: ACC/AHA 2017; Hazzard''s Ch. 37; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 88-year-old male named Paul has office blood pressure readings consistently above 150/80 mmHg across 3 clinic visits. However, his home blood pressure log shows readings consistently below 125/75 mmHg. He has no target organ damage. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step to distinguish white coat hypertension from sustained (masked) hypertension?', '["Order 24-hour ambulatory blood pressure monitoring (ABPM); ABPM is the gold standard for diagnosing white coat hypertension, confirming true mean BP, and identifying nocturnal dipping or non-dipping patterns", "Prescribe an anxiolytic (benzodiazepine) before each clinic visit to eliminate the white coat effect and obtain a true reading", "Dismiss the elevated readings entirely and discontinue monitoring because the patient reports no symptoms", "Immediately start chlorthalidone based on the elevated clinic readings alone, as office BP is always more accurate than home measurements", "Refer for urgent cardiac catheterization to evaluate for coronary artery disease as the cause of the elevated readings"]'::jsonb, 0, '**White coat hypertension (WCH)** occurs in 15-30% of older adults and is defined as elevated BP in the clinical setting (>= 130/80 mmHg) with normal out-of-office BP (< 130/80 by ABPM or < 135/85 by home BP). **24-hour ambulatory blood pressure monitoring (ABPM)** is the **gold standard** for: (1) Diagnosing white coat hypertension (elevated office, normal ambulatory), (2) Detecting **masked hypertension** (normal office, elevated ambulatory — which carries high cardiovascular risk), (3) Evaluating **nocturnal dipping** (normal >= 10% SBP drop at night; non-dipping is an independent CV risk factor). WCH carries lower cardiovascular risk than sustained hypertension, but still requires periodic monitoring. (Ref: ACC/AHA 2017; Hazzard''s Ch. 37; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
