    (v_topic_id, 'A 72-year-old male named Harry has a 2-year history of poorly controlled isolated systolic hypertension (SBP consistently > 160 mmHg). He asks about the most serious consequence of untreated ISH. Based on epidemiological evidence, which of the following is the most accurate counseling statement?', '["ISH only increases the risk of hemorrhagic stroke; ischemic stroke risk is determined exclusively by atrial fibrillation status", "Isolated systolic hypertension is the strongest modifiable risk factor for both ischemic and hemorrhagic stroke in older adults; the SHEP trial demonstrated that treating ISH with low-dose chlorthalidone reduced stroke risk by 36%", "Only diastolic hypertension (DBP > 90) confers stroke risk; isolated elevation of systolic pressure is benign", "ISH increases the risk of peripheral vascular disease but has no independent association with cerebrovascular events", "Treatment of ISH in adults over 70 provides no stroke prevention benefit according to all major randomized trials"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Isolated systolic hypertension is the strongest modifiable risk factor for stroke** in older adults. The landmark **SHEP (Systolic Hypertension in the Elderly Program) trial** randomized 4,736 adults aged >= 60 with ISH (SBP 160-219, DBP < 90) to low-dose chlorthalidone vs. placebo and demonstrated: (1) **36% reduction in total stroke**, (2) **27% reduction in coronary events**, (3) **32% reduction in heart failure**. Additional trials (Syst-Eur, HYVET) confirmed these benefits across the age spectrum, including the very elderly (> 80 years). Every 10 mmHg reduction in SBP reduces stroke risk by approximately 30-40%. (Ref: SHEP Trial, JAMA 1991; Syst-Eur Trial; HYVET Trial; Hazzard''s Ch. 37)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 84-year-old female named Ruby has a 3-year history of poorly controlled isolated systolic hypertension (SBP consistently > 160 mmHg). She asks about the most serious consequence of untreated ISH. Based on epidemiological evidence, which of the following is the most accurate counseling statement?', '["Isolated systolic hypertension is the strongest modifiable risk factor for both ischemic and hemorrhagic stroke in older adults; the SHEP trial demonstrated that treating ISH with low-dose chlorthalidone reduced stroke risk by 36%", "Only diastolic hypertension (DBP > 90) confers stroke risk; isolated elevation of systolic pressure is benign", "ISH only increases the risk of hemorrhagic stroke; ischemic stroke risk is determined exclusively by atrial fibrillation status", "Treatment of ISH in adults over 70 provides no stroke prevention benefit according to all major randomized trials", "ISH increases the risk of peripheral vascular disease but has no independent association with cerebrovascular events"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Isolated systolic hypertension is the strongest modifiable risk factor for stroke** in older adults. The landmark **SHEP (Systolic Hypertension in the Elderly Program) trial** randomized 4,736 adults aged >= 60 with ISH (SBP 160-219, DBP < 90) to low-dose chlorthalidone vs. placebo and demonstrated: (1) **36% reduction in total stroke**, (2) **27% reduction in coronary events**, (3) **32% reduction in heart failure**. Additional trials (Syst-Eur, HYVET) confirmed these benefits across the age spectrum, including the very elderly (> 80 years). Every 10 mmHg reduction in SBP reduces stroke risk by approximately 30-40%. (Ref: SHEP Trial, JAMA 1991; Syst-Eur Trial; HYVET Trial; Hazzard''s Ch. 37)'),
