    (v_topic_id, 'A 84-year-old female named Phyllis has a 3-year history of poorly controlled isolated systolic hypertension (SBP consistently > 160 mmHg). She asks about the most serious consequence of untreated ISH. Based on epidemiological evidence, which of the following is the most accurate counseling statement?', '["ISH only increases the risk of hemorrhagic stroke; ischemic stroke risk is determined exclusively by atrial fibrillation status", "Treatment of ISH in adults over 70 provides no stroke prevention benefit according to all major randomized trials", "Isolated systolic hypertension is the strongest modifiable risk factor for both ischemic and hemorrhagic stroke in older adults; the SHEP trial demonstrated that treating ISH with low-dose chlorthalidone reduced stroke risk by 36%", "ISH increases the risk of peripheral vascular disease but has no independent association with cerebrovascular events", "Only diastolic hypertension (DBP > 90) confers stroke risk; isolated elevation of systolic pressure is benign"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Isolated systolic hypertension is the strongest modifiable risk factor for stroke** in older adults. The landmark **SHEP (Systolic Hypertension in the Elderly Program) trial** randomized 4,736 adults aged >= 60 with ISH (SBP 160-219, DBP < 90) to low-dose chlorthalidone vs. placebo and demonstrated: (1) **36% reduction in total stroke**, (2) **27% reduction in coronary events**, (3) **32% reduction in heart failure**. Additional trials (Syst-Eur, HYVET) confirmed these benefits across the age spectrum, including the very elderly (> 80 years). Every 10 mmHg reduction in SBP reduces stroke risk by approximately 30-40%. (Ref: SHEP Trial, JAMA 1991; Syst-Eur Trial; HYVET Trial; Hazzard''s Ch. 37)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 96-year-old male named George is started on chlorthalidone for isolated systolic hypertension. Two weeks later, he presents with confusion, nausea, and a serum sodium of 130 mEq/L (reference: 136-145). Which of the following best describes the mechanism of thiazide-induced hyponatremia in older adults and why they are particularly vulnerable?', '["Thiazides inhibit sodium reabsorption at the Na-Cl cotransporter in the distal convoluted tubule while preserving free water absorption in the collecting duct (via ADH-mediated aquaporin channels); older adults are predisposed due to age-related impaired free water excretion, increased ADH sensitivity, decreased total body water, and lower muscle mass", "Hyponatremia is only a side effect of loop diuretics and never occurs with thiazide diuretics in clinical practice", "Thiazides cause massive sodium wasting in the loop of Henle by blocking the Na+/K+/2Cl- cotransporter, identical to loop diuretics", "Thiazides increase ADH secretion from the posterior pituitary by directly stimulating vasopressin V2 receptors on the renal collecting duct", "Thiazide-induced hyponatremia is caused by excessive sweating and insensible water losses, not by a renal mechanism"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Thiazide-induced hyponatremia** is one of the most dangerous adverse effects in older adults. Mechanism: Thiazides block the **Na-Cl cotransporter (NCC)** in the **distal convoluted tubule (DCT)**, causing sodium loss. However, unlike loop diuretics (which impair the concentrating mechanism and promote free water excretion), thiazides **preserve the medullary concentration gradient** and **do not impair ADH-mediated free water reabsorption** in the collecting duct. This leads to a **dilutional hyponatremia**. Older adults are especially vulnerable because: (1) **Reduced total body water and lean mass**, (2) **Increased baseline ADH sensitivity**, (3) **Decreased renal free water clearance**, (4) **Concomitant SSRIs or NSAIDs** that worsen SIADH. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 37; AGS Beers Criteria 2023; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 78-year-old male named Robert is started on indapamide for isolated systolic hypertension. Two weeks later, he presents with confusion, nausea, and a serum sodium of 134 mEq/L (reference: 136-145). Which of the following best describes the mechanism of thiazide-induced hyponatremia in older adults and why they are particularly vulnerable?', '["Hyponatremia is only a side effect of loop diuretics and never occurs with thiazide diuretics in clinical practice", "Thiazides cause massive sodium wasting in the loop of Henle by blocking the Na+/K+/2Cl- cotransporter, identical to loop diuretics", "Thiazide-induced hyponatremia is caused by excessive sweating and insensible water losses, not by a renal mechanism", "Thiazides inhibit sodium reabsorption at the Na-Cl cotransporter in the distal convoluted tubule while preserving free water absorption in the collecting duct (via ADH-mediated aquaporin channels); older adults are predisposed due to age-related impaired free water excretion, increased ADH sensitivity, decreased total body water, and lower muscle mass", "Thiazides increase ADH secretion from the posterior pituitary by directly stimulating vasopressin V2 receptors on the renal collecting duct"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Thiazide-induced hyponatremia** is one of the most dangerous adverse effects in older adults. Mechanism: Thiazides block the **Na-Cl cotransporter (NCC)** in the **distal convoluted tubule (DCT)**, causing sodium loss. However, unlike loop diuretics (which impair the concentrating mechanism and promote free water excretion), thiazides **preserve the medullary concentration gradient** and **do not impair ADH-mediated free water reabsorption** in the collecting duct. This leads to a **dilutional hyponatremia**. Older adults are especially vulnerable because: (1) **Reduced total body water and lean mass**, (2) **Increased baseline ADH sensitivity**, (3) **Decreased renal free water clearance**, (4) **Concomitant SSRIs or NSAIDs** that worsen SIADH. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 37; AGS Beers Criteria 2023; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
